# shir_nodes/map_literal.node — MapLiteral: a whole dict VALUE in
# expression position (`map[K]V{...}` composite literal, JS object
# literal, Zig/Java map constructors).
#
# GENERIC. keys[i] / values[i] are parallel lists (equal length);
# non-string keys stringify exactly like the host object model does.
# A named map built statement-by-statement (assocSet on a variable)
# stays the existing A1 shape — THIS node is only for the anonymous
# expression-position form that previously had no drop-in shape.
#
# Requires build.rs DSL field type `exprs` (Vec<IrExpr>) added for
# list-bearing nodes.

node MapLiteral
tag "MapLiteral"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field keys: exprs
field values: exprs
# Spec: TextOps#map_literal_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "MapLiteral: a dict literal over parallel key/value lists"). The renderer is the
# specification: with `length keys = length values` it emits an ObjectExpression of
# the zipped pairs in order, keys `computed`; otherwise it refuses and the sh2.*
# fallback runs. The theorem models the node as an association list of byte pairs plus
# an ABSTRACT key-stringification `st` (one function standing for the host object
# model, which stringifies at runtime), and proves, for every st and query q: the
# entries are exactly `zip keys values` (so `min` of the two lengths); the structural
# law `map_lookup st q (ps @ [(k, v)]) = (if st k = st q then Some v else map_lookup st
# q ps)` — a DUPLICATE key is therefore last-wins, as a JS object literal is; a list
# whose keys all stringify away from q answers `None`; and both corollaries on
# `zip (ks @ [k]) (vs @ [v])`.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): the stringification rule itself (numbers to
# decimal text, `true`/`false`, `-0` to "0", `NaN`, objects to "[object Object]"-like
# text) — so two DISTINCT key expressions may collide, which is why last-wins is
# stated rather than "keys are unique"; property ORDER and iteration (JS reorders
# integer-like property names, so the laws are about lookup, never "the i-th
# property"); key/value expression evaluation order and side effects (the model takes
# already-evaluated bytes); the length-mismatch refusal (the renderer's refuse>guess
# guard whose alternative is the unchanged fallback); and encoding beyond bytes (no
# Unicode normalization or locale rules).
spec: TextOps#map_literal_sound
